[s0l0!-header][h1]Conditional Compilation Directives[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

Conditional compilation of segments of code is controlled by a set of directives: [b]#if[/b], [b]#else[/b], [b]#elif[/b], and [b]#endif[/b].

[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header][code format="c" codeId="2217"]#define LEVEL 4

int main() {
  #if LEVEL > 6
    /* do something */
  #elif LEVEL > 5
    /* else if branch */
  #elif LEVEL > 4
    /* another else if */
  #else
    /* last option here */
  #endif

  return 0;
} [/code]
There are instances where such conditional compilation can be useful, but this type of code should be used sparingly.
The [b]defined()[/b] preprocessor operator can be used with #if, as in:[code format="c" ]#if !defined(LEVEL)
  /* statements */
#endif[/code]
The [b]#if[/b] and if statement are not interchangeable. The #if is evaluated using data available to the preprocessor, which then sends only the true branch for compilation. 
[note]An if statement uses data provided at runtime with the possibility of branching to any else clause.[/note]

